  "walkthroughs": [
    {
      "tag": "Theory",
      "h2": "Motif and Theme",
      "body_html": "<p>A <strong>motif</strong> is the smallest recognisable idea \u2014 Beethoven's four-note opening to Symphony No. 5 (1808) is the textbook example. A <strong>theme</strong> is a longer melody, often built from motifs.</p>"
    },
    {
      "tag": "Techniques",
      "h2": "Six Transformations",
      "body_html": "<p>Common developments: <strong>sequence</strong> (transpose), <strong>inversion</strong> (intervals reversed), <strong>retrograde</strong> (backwards), <strong>augmentation</strong> (longer), <strong>diminution</strong> (shorter), <strong>fragmentation</strong> (break apart).</p>"
    },
    {
      "tag": "Application",
      "h2": "In Practice",
      "body_html": "<p>Apply each technique to your own motif during the compositional process. Examiners reward clear evidence of <strong>development</strong>, not just statement of ideas.</p>"
    }
  ],
